.TH BASIC 1
.SH NAME
basic \- line-numbered BASIC interpreter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B basic
.I file.bas
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Basic
is a work-alike of the BASIC named in the languages chapter of
Nelson's
.IR "Computer Lib/Dream Machines" :
line-numbered, imperative, built around PRINT/INPUT/GOTO/FOR rather
than TRAC's string rewriting or APL's array notation. Every line
starts with a number, its own address for GOTO/GOSUB; statements run
top to bottom in line-number order unless control flow says otherwise,
and there is no structure beyond what GOTO/GOSUB/FOR give.
.PP
Statements:
.B LET
(keyword optional \(em
.B "X = 5"
works the same as
.BR "LET X = 5" ),
.B PRINT
(comma tabs, trailing
.B ;
suppresses the newline, including across separate PRINT statements),
.B INPUT
(with an optional prompt string),
.BR "IF ... THEN" ,
.BR GOTO ,
.BR GOSUB / RETURN ,
.BR FOR / TO / STEP / NEXT ,
.BR END ,
.BR REM .
.PP
Expressions support
.BR + - * / MOD ,
string concatenation via
.B +
when either side is a string, the six comparisons
.RB ( = <> < > <= >= ),
parentheses, unary minus, and five built-in functions:
.BR LEN ,
.BR VAL ,
.BR STR$ ,
.BR ABS ,
.BR INT .
A trailing
.B $
on a variable name makes it a string variable, the universal
convention every classic BASIC used.
.PP
Not implemented, a deliberate scope cut: arrays/DIM, DEF FN, functions
beyond the five above, and doubled-quote string escaping (a string
literal cannot contain a literal
.B '"'
at all).
.SH FILES
.TF /lib/basic/fizzbuzz.bas
.TP
.B /lib/basic/fizzbuzz.bas
FOR/MOD/IF-THEN/string-building.
.TP
.B /lib/basic/greet.bas
INPUT with a prompt, GOSUB/RETURN, LEN.
.TP
.B /lib/basic/features.bas
STEP, all four ordering comparisons, ABS/VAL/INT.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/basic.b
